WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.273
This lecture uses one problem, sorting

00:00:03.349 --> 00:00:06.245
to ask the 3 questions we will ask

00:00:06.322 --> 00:00:09.142
about every algorithm in this course

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:11.962
how does it work, why is it correct

00:00:11.962 --> 00:00:14.020
and how long does it take.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.605
The input is a sequence of n numbers.

00:00:17.605 --> 00:00:20.752
The output is a reordering of them

00:00:20.752 --> 00:00:22.938
in non-decreasing order

00:00:22.938 --> 00:00:26.698
and it must be a permutation of the input

00:00:26.698 --> 00:00:30.108
sorting 3, 1, 2 into 1, 1

00:00:30.108 --> 00:00:32.556
2 is in order, but wrong.

00:00:32.556 --> 00:00:36.490
A value was lost, and another appears twice.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.972
The right answer is 1, 2, 3

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:41.451
the same values

00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:44.496
each as many times as it appeared.

00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:47.627
And the test is less than or equal

00:00:47.627 --> 00:00:48.932
not less than

00:00:48.932 --> 00:00:52.760
because the input may contain equal values.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.030
The numbers being sorted are called keys.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.668
Our input is an array

00:00:57.739 --> 00:00:59.876
so we can reach any element by

00:00:59.947 --> 00:01:01.870
its index in constant time.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.526
Insertion sort is a simple algorithm that works

00:01:05.592 --> 00:01:07.948
well for a small number of elements.

00:01:08.014 --> 00:01:10.174
It sorts the way most people sort

00:01:10.239 --> 00:01:11.810
a hand of playing cards.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.690
Here are 6 cards, the textbook's example.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.862
Your left hand holds the

00:01:16.937 --> 00:01:18.683
cards picked up so far

00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:20.505
always in sorted order.

00:01:20.505 --> 00:01:23.768
At first it holds just 1 card, the 5.

00:01:23.768 --> 00:01:26.880
Then you pick up the next card, the 2.

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.394
To find its place

00:01:28.394 --> 00:01:31.238
you compare it with the cards in your hand

00:01:31.238 --> 00:01:32.531
from right to left

00:01:32.531 --> 00:01:35.052
until you find one that is not larger.

00:01:35.052 --> 00:01:37.703
Every larger card moves 1 place right

00:01:37.703 --> 00:01:40.030
and the new card goes into the gap.

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.310
In an array, the left hand is the prefix

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:46.570
a[0..j-1]

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:49.420
and the next card is a[j].

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.966
Here is the textbook's pseudocode.

00:01:52.966 --> 00:01:55.671
It numbers the array from 1

00:01:55.671 --> 00:01:59.160
so the outer loop runs j from 2 to n.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.778
It copies A[j] into key

00:02:01.778 --> 00:02:04.394
and starts i just left of it

00:02:04.394 --> 00:02:05.790
at j - 1.

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.235
While i is still inside the array

00:02:09.235 --> 00:02:12.148
and A[i] is greater than key

00:02:12.148 --> 00:02:15.151
it moves A[i] 1 place right

00:02:15.151 --> 00:02:17.700
and steps i one place left.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:19.510
When the loop stops

00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:21.991
the hole at i + 1 is exactly

00:02:22.064 --> 00:02:23.377
where key belongs

00:02:23.377 --> 00:02:25.567
and line eight puts it there.

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:27.610
Now the same thing in Java.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.862
Java numbers arrays from zero

00:02:30.862 --> 00:02:34.602
so the outer loop starts at j = 1

00:02:34.691 --> 00:02:37.273
and the inner loop stops when

00:02:37.362 --> 00:02:39.054
i falls below zero.

00:02:39.054 --> 00:02:42.260
The test reads: i is at least zero

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.110
and a[i] is greater than key.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:48.288
The order of those two tests matters.

00:02:48.288 --> 00:02:51.346
In Java, the and operator stops as soon

00:02:51.422 --> 00:02:53.486
as the first test is false

00:02:53.486 --> 00:02:55.321
so when i is -1

00:02:55.321 --> 00:02:57.614
a[-1] is never read.

00:02:57.614 --> 00:02:59.830
Written the other way round

00:02:59.907 --> 00:03:02.429
the program throws an array index

00:03:02.506 --> 00:03:04.340
out of bounds exception

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.780
as soon as key is the smallest value so far.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:11.970
So check the index first, then the value.

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:15.928
Let's run the Java method on 5, 2

00:03:15.928 --> 00:03:17.921
4, 6, 1, 3

00:03:17.921 --> 00:03:20.086
and watch the variables.

00:03:20.086 --> 00:03:23.898
The first element alone is a sorted prefix

00:03:23.898 --> 00:03:25.630
so j starts at 1.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.590
key gets a[j], the 2

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:32.140
which leaves a hole at index 1.

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:36.420
And i starts at j - 1, index 0.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.406
The test: i is at least zero

00:03:39.406 --> 00:03:42.134
and 5 is greater than 2.

00:03:42.134 --> 00:03:45.043
So 5 shifts one place right

00:03:45.043 --> 00:03:48.770
into the hole, and i drops to -1.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:51.074
Now i is below zero

00:03:51.074 --> 00:03:53.713
so the loop stops at once.

00:03:53.811 --> 00:03:56.938
The last line drops key into the

00:03:57.035 --> 00:04:00.553
hole at a[i + 1]: index 0.

00:04:00.553 --> 00:04:03.680
The sorted prefix is 2, 5.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:07.220
The trace program in the notes prints

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.380
the array after each pass

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:12.900
the bar marks the end of the sorted prefix.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.060
Pass 2: the key is 4.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:17.977
Compare with 5: larger

00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:20.149
so 5 shifts right.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:23.505
Then 2 is not larger than 4

00:04:23.505 --> 00:04:25.381
so the loop stops

00:04:25.381 --> 00:04:29.724
and 4 drops in between: 2, 4, 5.

00:04:29.724 --> 00:04:30.810
One shift.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.389
Pass 3: the key is 6.

00:04:33.389 --> 00:04:35.782
5 is not larger than 6

00:04:35.782 --> 00:04:38.011
so the test fails at once

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:41.394
and 6 goes straight back where it was.

00:04:41.394 --> 00:04:42.880
No shifts at all.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.631
Follow pass 4 closely.

00:04:45.631 --> 00:04:47.020
The key is 1

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:50.666
and the sorted prefix is 2, 4, 5

00:04:50.666 --> 00:04:51.166
6.

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.410
All four are larger than 1, so 6 shifts

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:59.190
5 shifts, 4 shifts, and 2 shifts

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.530
and i falls to -1.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.396
The key goes into a[0]: four shifts

00:05:05.396 --> 00:05:09.100
because 1 is the smallest value so far.

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.760
The last pass inserts 3.

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:14.730
The key is compared from the right

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.577
6 shifts, 5 shifts, 4 shifts

00:05:18.577 --> 00:05:20.751
and 2 is not larger

00:05:20.751 --> 00:05:23.397
so 3 drops in after it.

00:05:23.397 --> 00:05:24.720
Three shifts.

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:29.671
The whole array is sorted: 1, 2, 3

00:05:29.671 --> 00:05:31.530
4, 5, 6.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:33.138
Now you try.

00:05:33.138 --> 00:05:36.223
Trace insertion sort on 31

00:05:36.223 --> 00:05:39.222
41, 59, 26

00:05:39.222 --> 00:05:41.278
41, 58.

00:05:41.278 --> 00:05:43.506
There are two 41s

00:05:43.506 --> 00:05:46.590
call them the first and the second.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:49.573
How many shifts does each pass make?

00:05:49.646 --> 00:05:52.279
And does the second 41 end up

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:54.546
before or after the first one?

00:05:54.546 --> 00:05:56.740
Pause the video and trace it.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.992
Passes 1 and 2 make no shifts

00:06:02.992 --> 00:06:05.899
41 is larger than 31

00:06:05.899 --> 00:06:09.120
and 59 is larger than 41.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.758
Pass 3 inserts 26.

00:06:11.758 --> 00:06:14.045
It is smaller than all three

00:06:14.045 --> 00:06:16.638
so all three shift: three shifts

00:06:16.638 --> 00:06:19.230
and 26 goes to the front.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.448
Pass 4 is the second 41.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:24.050
59 shifts.

00:06:24.135 --> 00:06:26.834
Then the test compares 41

00:06:26.918 --> 00:06:28.183
with 41.

00:06:28.183 --> 00:06:31.304
Is 41 greater than 41?

00:06:31.304 --> 00:06:31.641
No.

00:06:31.641 --> 00:06:35.352
So the loop stops, and the second 41

00:06:35.437 --> 00:06:37.292
stays after the first.

00:06:37.292 --> 00:06:38.220
One shift.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:41.873
Pass 5 inserts 58

00:06:41.873 --> 00:06:44.515
only 59 shifts.

00:06:44.515 --> 00:06:49.140
So the shifts are 0, 0, 3, 1

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:49.690
1.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.278
And the two 41s are still

00:06:52.348 --> 00:06:54.026
in their original order.

00:06:54.026 --> 00:06:57.102
A sort with this property is called stable

00:06:57.102 --> 00:06:59.200
and insertion sort is stable.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.231
The reason is the test: greater than

00:07:02.231 --> 00:07:04.187
not greater than or equal.

00:07:04.187 --> 00:07:07.230
An element never jumps over an equal one.

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.157
Insertion sort works in place

00:07:10.157 --> 00:07:12.112
apart from key, i and j

00:07:12.112 --> 00:07:14.067
it uses no extra memory

00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:16.570
whatever the size of the array.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.682
Two common mistakes.

00:07:18.682 --> 00:07:22.471
The first: swapping a[i] and

00:07:22.559 --> 00:07:24.497
a[i + 1] in the inner loop.

00:07:24.497 --> 00:07:25.907
It still sorts

00:07:25.907 --> 00:07:29.079
but each swap is three assignments

00:07:29.079 --> 00:07:31.017
where a shift is one.

00:07:31.017 --> 00:07:33.749
Keep key in a variable, shift

00:07:33.749 --> 00:07:36.480
and write key once at the end.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:37.600
The second

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.540
starting the outer loop at j = 0.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:42.430
The first element alone is

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.180
already a sorted prefix

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.120
so the loop starts at the second element

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.660
j = 1 in Java

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.550
and 2 in the pseudocode.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:51.898
To sum up.

00:07:51.898 --> 00:07:55.200
1: Insertion sort keeps a sorted prefix

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.118
and inserts the next element into it

00:07:58.195 --> 00:08:00.345
shifting the larger elements

00:08:00.422 --> 00:08:01.650
1 place right.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:02.383
2:

00:08:02.383 --> 00:08:06.591
the test checks the index before the value.

00:08:06.591 --> 00:08:10.800
3: It sorts in place, and it is stable.

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:15.435
Try this yourself: insertion sort on 4,

00:08:15.435 --> 00:08:19.619
3, 2, 10, 12, 1, 5, 6.

00:08:19.619 --> 00:08:22.986
Write the array after each pass

00:08:22.986 --> 00:08:25.230
and count the shifts.

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:28.860
In part 2, we prove that insertion sort

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.020
is correct on every input

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:33.340
and count how long it takes.

